{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfxFwBuo8B2UtDSDtVppu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SuYtEWpk3TNfeXxTtimRv5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR. The spelling is hash-free.\nSo `[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part of\nits key, and so is each cell's number of occurrences in the whole task\n(the one structural invariant that separates `#{[A B] [B C]}`,\n`[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` and the like without a search; appendix A measures it).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\nindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\nwhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\nnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n  the count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's bound of\n  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`: 128 per scalar and per\n  container, 4 per character, 16 plus length per byte payload), which\n  bounds the encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by\n  reference, so a spelling's own size is its spelled positions. Hence\n  every byte the procedure allocates for a comparison is charged before\n  the allocation, and the bytes alive at any time are bounded by the\n  budget plus the input. No polynomial bound is claimed; the budget is\n  the bound. Default `4194304` (it takes over the scale of the deleted\n  `:label-bytes`; appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300 generated states,\n  544554 for a 65536-character stream descriptor, 37210 for the pinned\n  fixture). A literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest\n  set or map exceeds it (6a's `:label-bytes` allowed about 8000); in an\n  ordered position a literal is never spelled and costs one unit.\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n### 1.6 Totality\n\nThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\nimplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\ncensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\nreaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\nmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\ncarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\nexpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n\n## 2. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\nwhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\nhost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\nqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\nconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\nbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n\n*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\nnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\nranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\ntheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\nso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\norder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\nstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\nfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\nisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\nfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\ncount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\nwhich the members agree).\n\n*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\nqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\nsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\nisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\ncompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n\n*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\nand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\ndistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\nthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\nmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\nagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\nsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\nthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\nthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\nall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n\n*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\nsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\nisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\n*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\nthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\nnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\nW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\na cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\nrefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\nand the budget bounds the count.\n\nConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\nenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\ncontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\nbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\nsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\ncross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n\n## 3. What is refused\n\nRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\nsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n\n- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n  alike).\n- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n  without a search.\n- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n\nEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\ndoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\nmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\ntwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\nrefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\nnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n\nMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\nlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\nelse 48 sampled orders):\n\n| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n\nEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\ndecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\nversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n\n**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\nfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n\n## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n\nAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\nlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\nbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\nseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\nand `:search`.\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n  added (`[:V :V]`).\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n  whole `emit` call.\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\nkeys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`).\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n  [:work]` under the default budget.\n\n## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n    frame fixtures.\n13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its\n    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with\n    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix\n    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with\n    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and\n    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every\n    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict\n    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is\n    reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §4 pass.\n21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor\n    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at\n    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`\n    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)\nand amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical\nnumbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure\nbelow, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\nenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\ncannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\nthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\ninterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\nnumbering assigned today.\"\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,\nentering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot\nchange the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\nenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\nrefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used\nonly inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an\nemitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the\nnumbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie\nrefusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\nrecomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\nby the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\nis exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics\nparagraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF\n§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and\ntwo `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\".\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit\ncounts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling\nis encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes\nhost enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as\ndefined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\nwalk)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →\n\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it\nconsumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream\nidentity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank\ninside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\nsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\nhost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\norder of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked\ntable in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in\nhost enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →\n\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft\n§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items\n12–22 of §5.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change.\n\n## 7. Owner questions\n\n1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its\n   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from\n   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a\n   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A\n   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random\n   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the\n   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states\n   numbered, different pins)?\n2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus\n   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?\n\n---\n\n# Appendices (non-normative)\n\n## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under\n`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure\nused for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering\nrestricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the\ncharging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation\nin §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing\ngroup is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of\nrefusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact\ninterchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when\nthere are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling\nfalse` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype\nis kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from\nthe probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match\n`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):\n\n```\nmise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's\nown tests pass unchanged in the same project, 21 tests, 324 assertions).\nRoots marked (6a) equal 6a's root for the same state.\n\n| State | Numbering | Root | `:work` |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `fab223…` (6a) | 37210 |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `2d9873a8…` (6a) | 3455 |\n| `#{X Y}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `07eda35d…` (6a) | 3185 |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`:159`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` (6a) | 1275 |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `da69c679…` (6a) / `49d4478c…` (6a) | 6197 / 5107 |\n| codex A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `387f294a…` (6a) | 6198 |\n| codex B `{X X, Y Z}` | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `be62ba43…` (6a) | 6212 |\n| codex C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`, and renamed | first listed inner set `cc-1, cc-2` | `18b70de6…` (6a) | 13831 |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `a850fce6…` (6a) | 3189 |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, z cc-3, w cc-4` | `e67405ec…` | 13835 |\n| witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | `b cc-1, a cc-2` | `0193312…` (6a) | 16564 |\n| `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | `x cc-1, y cc-2, z cc-3` | `d84480d4…` | 4848 |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | `a cc-1, b cc-2, c cc-3` | `726a9fbb…` | 6199 |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in order | `6e7c7a6b…` (6a) | 12413 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `0b9ef61e…` | 3836 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `ff124bfc…` | 3122 |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `y cc-1, x cc-2`; one child row shared; decodes | `09124ab9…` | — |\n| 400000-element literal beside a cell in a vector | numbers | `be7fc44e…` | 1273 |\n\nRefused (all `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`; verdict\nand path equal over 24 renamings plus shuffles; \"interchangeable\" is the\nexhaustive permissive verdict):\n\n| State | Path | Cells | Reason | Interchangeable | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `0fe898cc…` |\n| 3-cycle | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | `0b181cd4…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`:312`) | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `55ff168e…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | `[15]` | `a … g` | shared within group | **no** | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | `[15 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `92747c0f…` |\n| mixed bodies `#{[A B] [A C]}` | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | numbered |\n| anchored `[#{…} A]` | `[15 0]` | `b c d e` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` | `[15]` | `a b c d` | incomplete key | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]` | `[15 0 :member]` | `w x y z` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | `[15 :member 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| install child holding the 2-cycle | lift `[12 a 15]`; decode of an `emit-numbered` carrier: `:yin.k/undecodable`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | | | | |\n\nGates: two cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 cells under the\ndefault budget refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}`\nrefuses `[:depth]`; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at lift and, for a\nvalid carrier, at decode; the 400000-element literal inside a set refuses\n`[:work]`; an unreachable census cell is the defect\n`{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. The 65536-character descriptor state\nnumbers at `:work` 544554 (21290 at 128 characters; encoded comparison\nbytes 132540 against 1718, all charged before encoding).\n\nRandom search (seeded; each state compared with itself renamed and\nshuffled, and decoded through 6a's decoder, whose own retained\nrecomputation budget stops 397 of the carriers at `[:label-bytes]`: a\nprobe limitation, not a failure):\n\n| Generator | Numbered | Refused (interch. / not / undecided) | Only across groups | Count gain | Invariance fail | Decode ok / fail | max `:work` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values, 2000 | 1981 | 19 (16 / 3 / 0) | 7 | 20 | 0 | 1789 / 0 | 624926 |\n| frame-bearing, 200 | 196 | 4 (3 / 1 / 0) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 179 / 0 | 202939 |\n| with children, 100 | 98 | 2 (1 / 0 / 1) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 75 / 0 | 434068 |\n| incidence families, 1000 | 832 | 168 (92 / 17 / 59) | 74 | 295 | 0 | 667 / 0 | 453009 |\n\n\"Only across groups\" counts refusals whose every failing group failed\nonly clause (iii): an upper bound on what the component rule of appendix\nB could recover. \"Count gain\" counts states numbered only because the\nkey carries the occurrence count; the reverse count is 0 everywhere. A\ntest that simply entered ties in host order would have accepted the 21\nstates classified not interchangeable, with host-order-dependent rows:\nthat is why a sound test is needed at all.\n\nSize: the prototype's procedure region (`discovery.clj:79-416`) is 296\nnon-blank lines, of which roughly 80 are docstrings, trace and the two\ndiagnostic switches; 6a's `canonical-numbering` plus\n`check-label-bytes!` is 136 non-blank lines. In `rows.cljc` the\nreplacement reuses `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!`, the `size` bound and `emit-node`; expect about 200 added or\nreplacement lines and about 135 deleted, excluding tests.\n\n## B. Round 3's variants: components (D4), conflicts (D5) and the residue search (D6). Optional extension, not adopted\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`, 980\nlines, and `probe/discovery_r3.clj`) resolved surviving ties differently:\nlink tied members that share an unnumbered cell into **components**;\nencode each component by a local run over a synthetic\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` row; enter the components of least\nencoding; a component holding two members of one group is a **conflict**,\nrefused (D5) or resolved by **trials** (D6): one local run per member of\nthe duplicated group with that member entered first, the least complete\nspelling adopted, charged to `:search`. The base design of §1 keeps none\nof it: no component runs, no trials, no `:search` budget. What the\nextension would add over the base: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and the other\n\"shared across groups\" refusals (at most 82 of 3300 generated states,\nappendix A) via components, and with D6 every state of appendix A's\nrefused table, including the seven-cell state (11 trials) and 6a's own\nroots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle.\n\n*Proof sketch (round 3, lemmas C and E).* Equal complete component\nencodings are faithful spellings and exhibit a bijection under which the\ncomponents are the same structure group by group; since the cells of\ndistinct components are disjoint and confined, the block swap is an\nautomorphism, so entering a run of equal encodings in host order gives\nthe same rows. For D6, the multiset of trial spellings is canonical if\neach trial is, and equal least spellings give isomorphic numberings. The\nthird review found the induction incomplete: a trial starts from the\nsame numbering and the same cells as its parent, the forced member need\nnot number a cell before recursive work begins, and key runs under D6\nsearch instead of spelling residue; a well-founded measure covering key\nruns, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials\nwas never supplied. That, with the reviewer's judgement that D6 is not\nmaterially simpler than 6a, is why it is not adopted.\n\n*Measurements (round 3, `collab/numbering-probe/run-r3.log`).* Under D6:\n2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing and 100 with children all\nnumbered, 0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures, at most 13 trials\nand 1470 `:work` units (round-3 accounting, positions only); the 2-cycle\nand 3-cycle reached 6a's roots (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`), the loop plus\n2-cycle `858a891e…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…` in 11 trials and\n488 units; the reviewer's independent 5000-state run found no\ncanonicality counterexample in either mode and 2117 D5 refusals.\n\n## C. Why not portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation. Every candidate (branch `srvm-phase6a`,\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`: the task's id counter `gen-id` `:227-230`\nand `gensym` `:2312-2318`; the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions, `handle-cursor-unminted` `:557-568`, minted at `:2595-2602`;\na hash of occurrence and counter; a random id) names history, not\nstructure. Two tasks with structurally equal snapshots (two cursor cells\nwith equal bodies at equal positions, equal counters) carry different\nid sets when one created and dropped a third cursor and the other\nconsumed an unrelated fresh name; the cross-engine law would then make\nan execution trace part of the frame's identity, and lower would have to\nkeep a second global name beside its private resource entry. Discovery\norder gives the identity a structural name instead. Recommended against.\n\n## D. History\n\nRound 1 (2026-10-12): discovery order proposed; the orbit lemma was\nunsound (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested ties were never processed\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 2: alias-carrying keys, the fixpoint, the\ncomponent rule; the review found speculative key-run conflicts refusing\nwrongly and an install child's refusal escaping decode. Round 3: key\nruns never refuse, the child translation, rank spelling, the `:work`\nbudget, D6; the review found the byte accounting unimplemented, the D6\ninduction incomplete, the coverage paragraph wrong, the `:cursor-cells`\nand `:depth` gates dropped and the large-literal test incompatible with\ntotality. Round 4 (this text): the base design, with every one of those\nfindings addressed in §§1–4 and the variants moved here.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1110,"cache_read_input_tokens":260798,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1110},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52f48666-0c9e-4ba6-9ea9-34c287559857","uuid":"0b74c956-df24-4362-95db-d184ca212c47","timestamp":"2026-10-12T11:28:32.207Z","request_id":"req_011CfxFwBaTJm9p11GnLst1N","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SuYtEWpk3TNfeXxTtimRv5":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR. The spelling is hash-free.\nSo `[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part of\nits key, and so is each cell's number of occurrences in the whole task\n(the one structural invariant that separates `#{[A B] [B C]}`,\n`[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` and the like without a search; appendix A measures it).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\nindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\nwhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\nnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n  the count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's bound of\n  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`: 128 per scalar and per\n  container, 4 per character, 16 plus length per byte payload), which\n  bounds the encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by\n  reference, so a spelling's own size is its spelled positions. Hence\n  every byte the procedure allocates for a comparison is charged before\n  the allocation, and the bytes alive at any time are bounded by the\n  budget plus the input. No polynomial bound is claimed; the budget is\n  the bound. Default `4194304` (it takes over the scale of the deleted\n  `:label-bytes`; appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300 generated states,\n  544554 for a 65536-character stream descriptor, 37210 for the pinned\n  fixture). A literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest\n  set or map exceeds it (6a's `:label-bytes` allowed about 8000); in an\n  ordered position a literal is never spelled and costs one unit.\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n### 1.6 Totality\n\nThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\nimplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\ncensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\nreaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\nmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\ncarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\nexpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n\n## 2. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\nwhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\nhost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\nqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\nconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\nbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n\n*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\nnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\nranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\ntheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\nso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\norder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\nstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\nfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\nisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\nfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\ncount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\nwhich the members agree).\n\n*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\nqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\nsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\nisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\ncompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n\n*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\nand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\ndistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\nthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\nmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\nagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\nsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\nthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\nthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\nall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n\n*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\nsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\nisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\n*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\nthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\nnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\nW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\na cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\nrefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\nand the budget bounds the count.\n\nConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\nenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\ncontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\nbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\nsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\ncross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n\n## 3. What is refused\n\nRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\nsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n\n- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n  alike).\n- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n  without a search.\n- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n\nEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\ndoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\nmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\ntwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\nrefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\nnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n\nMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\nlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\nelse 48 sampled orders):\n\n| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n\nEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\ndecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\nversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n\n**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\nfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n\n## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n\nAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\nlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\nbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\nseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\nand `:search`.\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n  added (`[:V :V]`).\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n  whole `emit` call.\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\nkeys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`).\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n  [:work]` under the default budget.\n\n## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n    frame fixtures.\n13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its\n    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with\n    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix\n    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with\n    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and\n    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every\n    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict\n    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is\n    reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §4 pass.\n21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor\n    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at\n    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`\n    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)\nand amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical\nnumbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure\nbelow, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\nenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\ncannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\nthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\ninterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\nnumbering assigned today.\"\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,\nentering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot\nchange the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\nenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\nrefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used\nonly inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an\nemitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the\nnumbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie\nrefusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\nrecomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\nby the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\nis exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics\nparagraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF\n§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and\ntwo `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\".\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit\ncounts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling\nis encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes\nhost enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as\ndefined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\nwalk)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →\n\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it\nconsumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream\nidentity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank\ninside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\nsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\nhost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\norder of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked\ntable in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in\nhost enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →\n\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft\n§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items\n12–22 of §5.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change.\n\n## 7. Owner questions\n\n1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its\n   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from\n   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a\n   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A\n   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random\n   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the\n   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states\n   numbered, different pins)?\n2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus\n   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?\n\n---\n\n# Appendices (non-normative)\n\n## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under\n`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure\nused for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering\nrestricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the\ncharging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation\nin §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing\ngroup is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of\nrefusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact\ninterchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when\nthere are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling\nfalse` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype\nis kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from\nthe probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match\n`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):\n\n```\nmise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's\nown tests pass unchanged in the same project, 21 tests, 324 assertions).\nRoots marked (6a) equal 6a's root for the same state.\n\n| State | Numbering | Root | `:work` |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `fab223…` (6a) | 37210 |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `2d9873a8…` (6a) | 3455 |\n| `#{X Y}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `07eda35d…` (6a) | 3185 |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`:159`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` (6a) | 1275 |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `da69c679…` (6a) / `49d4478c…` (6a) | 6197 / 5107 |\n| codex A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `387f294a…` (6a) | 6198 |\n| codex B `{X X, Y Z}` | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `be62ba43…` (6a) | 6212 |\n| codex C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`, and renamed | first listed inner set `cc-1, cc-2` | `18b70de6…` (6a) | 13831 |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `a850fce6…` (6a) | 3189 |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, z cc-3, w cc-4` | `e67405ec…` | 13835 |\n| witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | `b cc-1, a cc-2` | `0193312…` (6a) | 16564 |\n| `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | `x cc-1, y cc-2, z cc-3` | `d84480d4…` | 4848 |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | `a cc-1, b cc-2, c cc-3` | `726a9fbb…` | 6199 |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in order | `6e7c7a6b…` (6a) | 12413 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `0b9ef61e…` | 3836 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `ff124bfc…` | 3122 |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `y cc-1, x cc-2`; one child row shared; decodes | `09124ab9…` | — |\n| 400000-element literal beside a cell in a vector | numbers | `be7fc44e…` | 1273 |\n\nRefused (all `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`; verdict\nand path equal over 24 renamings plus shuffles; \"interchangeable\" is the\nexhaustive permissive verdict):\n\n| State | Path | Cells | Reason | Interchangeable | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `0fe898cc…` |\n| 3-cycle | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | `0b181cd4…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`:312`) | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `55ff168e…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | `[15]` | `a … g` | shared within group | **no** | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | `[15 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `92747c0f…` |\n| mixed bodies `#{[A B] [A C]}` | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | numbered |\n| anchored `[#{…} A]` | `[15 0]` | `b c d e` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` | `[15]` | `a b c d` | incomplete key | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]` | `[15 0 :member]` | `w x y z` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | `[15 :member 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| install child holding the 2-cycle | lift `[12 a 15]`; decode of an `emit-numbered` carrier: `:yin.k/undecodable`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | | | | |\n\nGates: two cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 cells under the\ndefault budget refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}`\nrefuses `[:depth]`; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at lift and, for a\nvalid carrier, at decode; the 400000-element literal inside a set refuses\n`[:work]`; an unreachable census cell is the defect\n`{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. The 65536-character descriptor state\nnumbers at `:work` 544554 (21290 at 128 characters; encoded comparison\nbytes 132540 against 1718, all charged before encoding).\n\nRandom search (seeded; each state compared with itself renamed and\nshuffled, and decoded through 6a's decoder, whose own retained\nrecomputation budget stops 397 of the carriers at `[:label-bytes]`: a\nprobe limitation, not a failure):\n\n| Generator | Numbered | Refused (interch. / not / undecided) | Only across groups | Count gain | Invariance fail | Decode ok / fail | max `:work` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values, 2000 | 1981 | 19 (16 / 3 / 0) | 7 | 20 | 0 | 1789 / 0 | 624926 |\n| frame-bearing, 200 | 196 | 4 (3 / 1 / 0) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 179 / 0 | 202939 |\n| with children, 100 | 98 | 2 (1 / 0 / 1) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 75 / 0 | 434068 |\n| incidence families, 1000 | 832 | 168 (92 / 17 / 59) | 74 | 295 | 0 | 667 / 0 | 453009 |\n\n\"Only across groups\" counts refusals whose every failing group failed\nonly clause (iii): an upper bound on what the component rule of appendix\nB could recover. \"Count gain\" counts states numbered only because the\nkey carries the occurrence count; the reverse count is 0 everywhere. A\ntest that simply entered ties in host order would have accepted the 21\nstates classified not interchangeable, with host-order-dependent rows:\nthat is why a sound test is needed at all.\n\nSize: the prototype's procedure region (`discovery.clj:79-416`) is 296\nnon-blank lines, of which roughly 80 are docstrings, trace and the two\ndiagnostic switches; 6a's `canonical-numbering` plus\n`check-label-bytes!` is 136 non-blank lines. In `rows.cljc` the\nreplacement reuses `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!`, the `size` bound and `emit-node`; expect about 200 added or\nreplacement lines and about 135 deleted, excluding tests.\n\n## B. Round 3's variants: components (D4), conflicts (D5) and the residue search (D6). Optional extension, not adopted\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`, 980\nlines, and `probe/discovery_r3.clj`) resolved surviving ties differently:\nlink tied members that share an unnumbered cell into **components**;\nencode each component by a local run over a synthetic\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` row; enter the components of least\nencoding; a component holding two members of one group is a **conflict**,\nrefused (D5) or resolved by **trials** (D6): one local run per member of\nthe duplicated group with that member entered first, the least complete\nspelling adopted, charged to `:search`. The base design of §1 keeps none\nof it: no component runs, no trials, no `:search` budget. What the\nextension would add over the base: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and the other\n\"shared across groups\" refusals (at most 82 of 3300 generated states,\nappendix A) via components, and with D6 every state of appendix A's\nrefused table, including the seven-cell state (11 trials) and 6a's own\nroots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle.\n\n*Proof sketch (round 3, lemmas C and E).* Equal complete component\nencodings are faithful spellings and exhibit a bijection under which the\ncomponents are the same structure group by group; since the cells of\ndistinct components are disjoint and confined, the block swap is an\nautomorphism, so entering a run of equal encodings in host order gives\nthe same rows. For D6, the multiset of trial spellings is canonical if\neach trial is, and equal least spellings give isomorphic numberings. The\nthird review found the induction incomplete: a trial starts from the\nsame numbering and the same cells as its parent, the forced member need\nnot number a cell before recursive work begins, and key runs under D6\nsearch instead of spelling residue; a well-founded measure covering key\nruns, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials\nwas never supplied. That, with the reviewer's judgement that D6 is not\nmaterially simpler than 6a, is why it is not adopted.\n\n*Measurements (round 3, `collab/numbering-probe/run-r3.log`).* Under D6:\n2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing and 100 with children all\nnumbered, 0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures, at most 13 trials\nand 1470 `:work` units (round-3 accounting, positions only); the 2-cycle\nand 3-cycle reached 6a's roots (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`), the loop plus\n2-cycle `858a891e…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…` in 11 trials and\n488 units; the reviewer's independent 5000-state run found no\ncanonicality counterexample in either mode and 2117 D5 refusals.\n\n## C. Why not portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation. Every candidate (branch `srvm-phase6a`,\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`: the task's id counter `gen-id` `:227-230`\nand `gensym` `:2312-2318`; the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions, `handle-cursor-unminted` `:557-568`, minted at `:2595-2602`;\na hash of occurrence and counter; a random id) names history, not\nstructure. Two tasks with structurally equal snapshots (two cursor cells\nwith equal bodies at equal positions, equal counters) carry different\nid sets when one created and dropped a third cursor and the other\nconsumed an unrelated fresh name; the cross-engine law would then make\nan execution trace part of the frame's identity, and lower would have to\nkeep a second global name beside its private resource entry. Discovery\norder gives the identity a structural name instead. Recommended against.\n\n## D. History\n\nRound 1 (2026-10-12): discovery order proposed; the orbit lemma was\nunsound (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested ties were never processed\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 2: alias-carrying keys, the fixpoint, the\ncomponent rule; the review found speculative key-run conflicts refusing\nwrongly and an install child's refusal escaping decode. Round 3: key\nruns never refuse, the child translation, rank spelling, the `:work`\nbudget, D6; the review found the byte accounting unimplemented, the D6\ninduction incomplete, the coverage paragraph wrong, the `:cursor-cells`\nand `:depth` gates dropped and the large-literal test incompatible with\ntotality. Round 4 (this text): the base design, with every one of those\nfindings addressed in §§1–4 and the variants moved here.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuYtEWpk3TNfeXxTtimRv5","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52f48666-0c9e-4ba6-9ea9-34c287559857","uuid":"b4104b1f-a881-4a1e-a011-e320a7dac80e","timestamp":"2026-10-12T11:28:32.307Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR. The spelling is hash-free.\nSo `[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part of\nits key, and so is each cell's number of occurrences in the whole task\n(the one structural invariant that separates `#{[A B] [B C]}`,\n`[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` and the like without a search; appendix A measures it).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\nindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\nwhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\nnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n  the count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's bound of\n  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`: 128 per scalar and per\n  container, 4 per character, 16 plus length per byte payload), which\n  bounds the encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by\n  reference, so a spelling's own size is its spelled positions. Hence\n  every byte the procedure allocates for a comparison is charged before\n  the allocation, and the bytes alive at any time are bounded by the\n  budget plus the input. No polynomial bound is claimed; the budget is\n  the bound. Default `4194304` (it takes over the scale of the deleted\n  `:label-bytes`; appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300 generated states,\n  544554 for a 65536-character stream descriptor, 37210 for the pinned\n  fixture). A literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest\n  set or map exceeds it (6a's `:label-bytes` allowed about 8000); in an\n  ordered position a literal is never spelled and costs one unit.\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n### 1.6 Totality\n\nThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\nimplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\ncensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\nreaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\nmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\ncarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\nexpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n\n## 2. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\nwhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\nhost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\nqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\nconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\nbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n\n*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\nnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\nranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\ntheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\nso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\norder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\nstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\nfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\nisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\nfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\ncount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\nwhich the members agree).\n\n*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\nqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\nsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\nisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\ncompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n\n*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\nand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\ndistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\nthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\nmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\nagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\nsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\nthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\nthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\nall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n\n*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\nsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\nisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\n*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\nthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\nnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\nW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\na cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\nrefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\nand the budget bounds the count.\n\nConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\nenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\ncontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\nbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\nsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\ncross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n\n## 3. What is refused\n\nRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\nsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n\n- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n  alike).\n- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n  without a search.\n- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n\nEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\ndoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\nmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\ntwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\nrefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\nnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n\nMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\nlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\nelse 48 sampled orders):\n\n| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n\nEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\ndecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\nversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n\n**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\nfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n\n## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n\nAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\nlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\nbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\nseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\nand `:search`.\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n  added (`[:V :V]`).\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n  whole `emit` call.\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\nkeys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`).\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n  [:work]` under the default budget.\n\n## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n    frame fixtures.\n13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its\n    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with\n    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix\n    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with\n    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and\n    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every\n    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict\n    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is\n    reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §4 pass.\n21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor\n    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at\n    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`\n    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)\nand amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical\nnumbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure\nbelow, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\nenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\ncannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\nthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\ninterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\nnumbering assigned today.\"\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,\nentering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot\nchange the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\nenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\nrefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used\nonly inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an\nemitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the\nnumbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie\nrefusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\nrecomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\nby the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\nis exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics\nparagraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF\n§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and\ntwo `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\".\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit\ncounts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling\nis encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes\nhost enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as\ndefined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\nwalk)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →\n\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it\nconsumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream\nidentity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank\ninside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\nsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\nhost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\norder of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked\ntable in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in\nhost enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →\n\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft\n§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items\n12–22 of §5.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change.\n\n## 7. Owner questions\n\n1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its\n   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from\n   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a\n   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A\n   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random\n   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the\n   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states\n   numbered, different pins)?\n2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus\n   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?\n\n---\n\n# Appendices (non-normative)\n\n## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under\n`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure\nused for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering\nrestricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the\ncharging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation\nin §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing\ngroup is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of\nrefusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact\ninterchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when\nthere are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling\nfalse` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype\nis kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from\nthe probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match\n`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):\n\n```\nmise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's\nown tests pass unchanged in the same project, 21 tests, 324 assertions).\nRoots marked (6a) equal 6a's root for the same state.\n\n| State | Numbering | Root | `:work` |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `fab223…` (6a) | 37210 |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `2d9873a8…` (6a) | 3455 |\n| `#{X Y}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `07eda35d…` (6a) | 3185 |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`:159`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` (6a) | 1275 |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `da69c679…` (6a) / `49d4478c…` (6a) | 6197 / 5107 |\n| codex A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `387f294a…` (6a) | 6198 |\n| codex B `{X X, Y Z}` | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `be62ba43…` (6a) | 6212 |\n| codex C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`, and renamed | first listed inner set `cc-1, cc-2` | `18b70de6…` (6a) | 13831 |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `a850fce6…` (6a) | 3189 |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, z cc-3, w cc-4` | `e67405ec…` | 13835 |\n| witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | `b cc-1, a cc-2` | `0193312…` (6a) | 16564 |\n| `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | `x cc-1, y cc-2, z cc-3` | `d84480d4…` | 4848 |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | `a cc-1, b cc-2, c cc-3` | `726a9fbb…` | 6199 |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in order | `6e7c7a6b…` (6a) | 12413 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `0b9ef61e…` | 3836 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `ff124bfc…` | 3122 |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `y cc-1, x cc-2`; one child row shared; decodes | `09124ab9…` | — |\n| 400000-element literal beside a cell in a vector | numbers | `be7fc44e…` | 1273 |\n\nRefused (all `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`; verdict\nand path equal over 24 renamings plus shuffles; \"interchangeable\" is the\nexhaustive permissive verdict):\n\n| State | Path | Cells | Reason | Interchangeable | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `0fe898cc…` |\n| 3-cycle | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | `0b181cd4…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`:312`) | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `55ff168e…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | `[15]` | `a … g` | shared within group | **no** | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | `[15 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `92747c0f…` |\n| mixed bodies `#{[A B] [A C]}` | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | numbered |\n| anchored `[#{…} A]` | `[15 0]` | `b c d e` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` | `[15]` | `a b c d` | incomplete key | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]` | `[15 0 :member]` | `w x y z` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | `[15 :member 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| install child holding the 2-cycle | lift `[12 a 15]`; decode of an `emit-numbered` carrier: `:yin.k/undecodable`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | | | | |\n\nGates: two cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 cells under the\ndefault budget refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}`\nrefuses `[:depth]`; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at lift and, for a\nvalid carrier, at decode; the 400000-element literal inside a set refuses\n`[:work]`; an unreachable census cell is the defect\n`{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. The 65536-character descriptor state\nnumbers at `:work` 544554 (21290 at 128 characters; encoded comparison\nbytes 132540 against 1718, all charged before encoding).\n\nRandom search (seeded; each state compared with itself renamed and\nshuffled, and decoded through 6a's decoder, whose own retained\nrecomputation budget stops 397 of the carriers at `[:label-bytes]`: a\nprobe limitation, not a failure):\n\n| Generator | Numbered | Refused (interch. / not / undecided) | Only across groups | Count gain | Invariance fail | Decode ok / fail | max `:work` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values, 2000 | 1981 | 19 (16 / 3 / 0) | 7 | 20 | 0 | 1789 / 0 | 624926 |\n| frame-bearing, 200 | 196 | 4 (3 / 1 / 0) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 179 / 0 | 202939 |\n| with children, 100 | 98 | 2 (1 / 0 / 1) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 75 / 0 | 434068 |\n| incidence families, 1000 | 832 | 168 (92 / 17 / 59) | 74 | 295 | 0 | 667 / 0 | 453009 |\n\n\"Only across groups\" counts refusals whose every failing group failed\nonly clause (iii): an upper bound on what the component rule of appendix\nB could recover. \"Count gain\" counts states numbered only because the\nkey carries the occurrence count; the reverse count is 0 everywhere. A\ntest that simply entered ties in host order would have accepted the 21\nstates classified not interchangeable, with host-order-dependent rows:\nthat is why a sound test is needed at all.\n\nSize: the prototype's procedure region (`discovery.clj:79-416`) is 296\nnon-blank lines, of which roughly 80 are docstrings, trace and the two\ndiagnostic switches; 6a's `canonical-numbering` plus\n`check-label-bytes!` is 136 non-blank lines. In `rows.cljc` the\nreplacement reuses `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!`, the `size` bound and `emit-node`; expect about 200 added or\nreplacement lines and about 135 deleted, excluding tests.\n\n## B. Round 3's variants: components (D4), conflicts (D5) and the residue search (D6). Optional extension, not adopted\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`, 980\nlines, and `probe/discovery_r3.clj`) resolved surviving ties differently:\nlink tied members that share an unnumbered cell into **components**;\nencode each component by a local run over a synthetic\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` row; enter the components of least\nencoding; a component holding two members of one group is a **conflict**,\nrefused (D5) or resolved by **trials** (D6): one local run per member of\nthe duplicated group with that member entered first, the least complete\nspelling adopted, charged to `:search`. The base design of §1 keeps none\nof it: no component runs, no trials, no `:search` budget. What the\nextension would add over the base: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and the other\n\"shared across groups\" refusals (at most 82 of 3300 generated states,\nappendix A) via components, and with D6 every state of appendix A's\nrefused table, including the seven-cell state (11 trials) and 6a's own\nroots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle.\n\n*Proof sketch (round 3, lemmas C and E).* Equal complete component\nencodings are faithful spellings and exhibit a bijection under which the\ncomponents are the same structure group by group; since the cells of\ndistinct components are disjoint and confined, the block swap is an\nautomorphism, so entering a run of equal encodings in host order gives\nthe same rows. For D6, the multiset of trial spellings is canonical if\neach trial is, and equal least spellings give isomorphic numberings. The\nthird review found the induction incomplete: a trial starts from the\nsame numbering and the same cells as its parent, the forced member need\nnot number a cell before recursive work begins, and key runs under D6\nsearch instead of spelling residue; a well-founded measure covering key\nruns, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials\nwas never supplied. That, with the reviewer's judgement that D6 is not\nmaterially simpler than 6a, is why it is not adopted.\n\n*Measurements (round 3, `collab/numbering-probe/run-r3.log`).* Under D6:\n2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing and 100 with children all\nnumbered, 0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures, at most 13 trials\nand 1470 `:work` units (round-3 accounting, positions only); the 2-cycle\nand 3-cycle reached 6a's roots (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`), the loop plus\n2-cycle `858a891e…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…` in 11 trials and\n488 units; the reviewer's independent 5000-state run found no\ncanonicality counterexample in either mode and 2117 D5 refusals.\n\n## C. Why not portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation. Every candidate (branch `srvm-phase6a`,\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`: the task's id counter `gen-id` `:227-230`\nand `gensym` `:2312-2318`; the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions, `handle-cursor-unminted` `:557-568`, minted at `:2595-2602`;\na hash of occurrence and counter; a random id) names history, not\nstructure. Two tasks with structurally equal snapshots (two cursor cells\nwith equal bodies at equal positions, equal counters) carry different\nid sets when one created and dropped a third cursor and the other\nconsumed an unrelated fresh name; the cross-engine law would then make\nan execution trace part of the frame's identity, and lower would have to\nkeep a second global name beside its private resource entry. Discovery\norder gives the identity a structural name instead. Recommended against.\n\n## D. History\n\nRound 1 (2026-10-12): discovery order proposed; the orbit lemma was\nunsound (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested ties were never processed\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 2: alias-carrying keys, the fixpoint, the\ncomponent rule; the review found speculative key-run conflicts refusing\nwrongly and an install child's refusal escaping decode. Round 3: key\nruns never refuse, the child translation, rank spelling, the `:work`\nbudget, D6; the review found the byte accounting unimplemented, the D6\ninduction incomplete, the coverage paragraph wrong, the `:cursor-cells`\nand `:depth` gates dropped and the large-literal test incompatible with\ntotality. Round 4 (this text): the base design, with every one of those\nfindings addressed in §§1–4 and the variants moved here.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":530,"newStart":1,"newLines":483,"lines":[" # Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)"," "," Status: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's","-refinement and search); not yet implemented.** Written 2026-10-12","-(Architect, claude-fable-5-1) for the owner's observation that \"the","-problem is not that the cursor cell has identity, but that the identity","-is local\", and the orchestrator's proposal to number cursor cells by","-**discovery order** in a canonical traversal. Revised twice the same day","-after independent reviews (gpt-6.1-sol,","-`collab/1792140000000-…findings.md` and `collab/1792170000000-…findings.md`).","-Round 2 repaired the orbit lemma (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested tie groups","-(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 3 (this text) fixes what the second review","-found: which local conflicts refuse (§2 D5, witness","-`[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`), the install-child refusal escaping decode, the","-work bound (now an explicit budget, with body labels spelled by rank so","-key bytes no longer grow with a stream descriptor), the A2 wording, the","-size estimate, and the coverage regression against 6a (2-cycles and","-3-cycles), for which §2 D6 specifies and §6 measures the repair. §8 lists","-what an implementation deletes, rewrites and keeps in 6a. Subordinate to","-[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md)","-(the carrier, body version 3) and","+refinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day","+(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after","+the third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with","+the round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not","+adopted. Subordinate to","+[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the","+carrier, body version 3) and"," [`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);"," vocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,","-cell\") exactly. Evidence is the carrier draft §5/§6 tables, slice 6a's","-`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`","-(branch `srvm-phase6a` at `d77da9f0`), and a prototype run against that","-code (§6). Nothing here is implemented.","+cell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`","+and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at","+`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the","+appendices are not."," ","-**In one line:** number cursor cells in the order a canonical traversal","-meets them (D1–D2); order the elements of a guest set or map by a key","-that is the spelling of a speculative local run over the element (so","-alias patterns such as `[X X]` versus `[Y Z]` are part of the key);","-defer equal keys and re-examine them to a fixpoint (D3); at a fixpoint,","-link tied elements that share an unnumbered cursor cell into components","-and enter the component of least complete encoding (D4); a component","-holding two equal-keyed elements of one container is the one case this","-cannot certify: refuse it (D5), or resolve it by a bounded residue search","-over that component alone (D6, recommended). Proof in §3, budget in §2","-\"Work\", results in §6, the cut against 6a in §8.","+**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in","+which a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is","+`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk","+is pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered","+except the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those","+the carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each","+member is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells","+already numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence","+count and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the","+members are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they","+are postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and","+re-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test","+proves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state","+unchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:","+sets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys."," ","-## 0. Vocabulary used below","+**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.","+In `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`","+occurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is","+entered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).","+In `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;","+the test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift","+refuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ","+(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`."," ","-- **Canonical traversal order** is the order D1 defines: a function of","-  the abstract task alone. **Host enumeration order** (also \"listed","-  order\") is the order in which a host happens to list the elements of a","-  guest set or the entries of a guest map, or the census; it is an","-  accident of the host. The procedure reads it in exactly one place, D4's","-  entry of components of equal encoding, where lemma C shows it cannot","-  change the result.","-- An **element** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry `[key value]`","-  (the key is a guest value and may be a cursor cell).","-- A **numbering** `N` is a partial map from the task's cursor cells to","-  positive integers, injective, assigned in increasing order; `|N|` is","-  its size. The emitted id of a cell numbered `k` is `:yin.k/cc-k`.","-- A **run** is one execution of the traversal-and-resolution procedure","-  over one item (a row or a value) from a starting numbering. The **root","-  run** is the run over the `:task` row from the empty numbering; its","-  numbers are the output. A **local run** is a run over one item from","-  the enclosing run's current numbering. There are three kinds of local","-  run, and the distinction decides what refuses (D5):","-  - a **key run** is a local run over one element, made to compute the","-    element's sort key; it is *speculative*: it never refuses, and","-    whatever it cannot resolve (a tie, a conflict) is spelled in its","-    key as `[:tie …]`;","-  - a **component run** is a local run over a component's synthetic","-    `[:component …]` row (D4); it must be *complete*, else D5;","-  - a **trial** (D6 only) is a component run that enters one chosen","-    element before its siblings.","-- A **body label** is `[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`,","-  a cursor cell's census body as plain data; its **rank** is its index","-  among the census's distinct body labels in bytewise order.","+## 1. The rule"," ","-## 1. Findings","+**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the","+procedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is","+over the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the","+output; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or","+map from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's","+**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the","+synthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of","+members of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**","+is the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity","+descriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise","+order; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task","+(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children","+are numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order."," ","-**F1. Every non-guest position is ordered.** From the carrier §5 tables","-(and `rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`), the positions that can hold a","-cursor-ref (`[:cursor-cell C]`) or a bare `C` slot, and their order:","+### 1.1 The transformation and the key"," ","-| Position | Order | Canonical index |","-|---|---|---|","-| `:task` slots 5 `free-env`, 6 `store`, 8 `parked`, 15 `result`; `:wait` state, pending; `:act` env, window, k; `:tail` k; `:kframe` env, window; `:reified` act; `:env` and `:closure` bindings; `:module-store` store; `:pending` `cursor-cell-C`, `response-cursor-cell-C`, `value-V`, `envelope-V` | fixed slot | slot index |","-| `:task` 7 `module-stores`, 12 `installs` | keyed by `M`, by module name | Jing bytes of the key; the child is a task boundary |","-| `:task` 13 `frames`; `:k`; `:vec`, `:list` elements | ordered vector | position `i` |","-| `:window` | keyed by register `N` | `N` ascending, numerically (carrier §5.2) |","-| `:bindings` (environments, closures, the free environment) | keyed by symbol `S` | Jing canonical bytes of `S` (§5 preamble: \"sorted\") |","-| `:store`, `:parked-table` | keyed by `D`, by `P` | bytes of the key; a parked-table value is a structural `:act`/`:tail` reference, entered like any row |","-| `:set` elements | **unordered** | none |","-| `:map` entries | **unordered** (keys are `V`) | none |","-| `:cursor-cells` census | not a position (carrier §6 step 2, §8 step 3) | — |","+When the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for","+its own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of","+a member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under","+the numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed","+from that one numbering):"," ","-So the only unordered positions are guest `:set` elements and guest","-`:map` entries. Both reviews confirmed the inventory complete. The","-example state in `collab/example-suspended-state.clj` holds its one","-cursor cell (`:cursor-1`) in three ordered positions: the act's","-environment under `c`, the window under register 2, and the `:next`","-pending's `cursor-cell-C` slot.","+- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell","+  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;","+- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**","+  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's","+  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its","+  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body","+  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;","+- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never","+  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,","+  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key","+  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as","+  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie","+  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as","+  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member."," ","-**F2. Discovery order subsumes the positions step.** The carrier's §6","-step 3 (positions) exists to let a cursor cell's number depend on where","-it occurs. A canonical traversal (D1) visits every ordered position in a","-fixed order, so \"the n-th cursor cell met\" already encodes the first","-position of each cursor cell; later positions cannot change an assigned","-number and need not be collected. Refinement (step 4) and search (step 5)","-existed only to turn position multisets into a total order; discovery","-order is a total order by construction.","+Keys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR. The spelling is hash-free.","+So `[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys","+`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part of","+its key, and so is each cell's number of occurrences in the whole task","+(the one structural invariant that separates `#{[A B] [B C]}`,","+`[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` and the like without a search; appendix A measures it)."," ","-**F3. Unordered containers are ordered by a content encoding that","-carries the alias pattern.** The first draft spelled an unnumbered cursor","-cell by its body alone, so `[X X]` and `[Y Z]` encoded alike. The","-repaired encoding (§2 \"Spelling\") numbers the cursor cells *inside* an","-element provisionally, in the element's own discovery order, relative to","-the enclosing numbering: `[X X]` spells `[(u0) (u0)]`, `[Y Z]` spells","-`[(u0) (u1)]`, so alias topology inside an element is part of its sort","-key, as a de Bruijn index is part of a term. The key of an element is the","-spelling produced by a *key run* over it, which applies the same rules","-recursively (nested containers sorted, nested ties deferred and","-re-examined, D4 inside) and spells what it cannot resolve as `[:tie …]`.","-Equal keys form a **tie group**, deferred to the run's worklist and","-re-examined to a fixpoint (D3) as other positions assign numbers. A","-provisional cell is spelled with its body *rank*, not its body label, so","-a key's size depends on the element's structure and not on the length","-of a stream descriptor (§2 \"Work\").","+### 1.2 The walk"," ","-**F4. What refuses, and what is lost against 6a.** What survives a run's","-fixpoint is a worklist of tie groups whose unnumbered cursor cells occur","-only inside tied elements. Link two tied elements when they share an","-unnumbered cursor cell; a connected set of tied elements is a","-**component**. Two cases:","-- *Certified*: no component holds two elements of one group. Every","-  component is then encoded by a component run over its elements in","-  group order (so a component spanning two groups, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`,","-  spells its shared cell once with one index), components are sorted by","-  that encoding, and the least run of equal encodings is entered in host","-  order. Equal encodings here mean *isomorphic* components (§3, lemma C).","-  `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`, `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` and","-  `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` are this case.","-- *Conflict*: some component holds two elements of one tie group. Then","-  entering either element first is a choice D1–D4 cannot show to be","-  harmless. **Which runs refuse.** Only the *root run* and *component","-  runs* refuse on a conflict (D5). A *key run* never refuses: it is a","-  speculation made to sort, its unresolved residue is spelled `[:tie …]`","-  in the key, and the enclosing run may resolve the same elements later.","-  The second review's witness is exactly this: in `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`","-  the key run over the inner set reaches a conflict (`[A B]` and `[B A]`","-  tie and share both cells), the outer set has one element and is","-  entered, the inner tie is deferred to the root worklist, index 1 meets","-  `A = cc-1`, and the root fixpoint separates `[A B]` (`[(n 1) (u0)]`)","-  from `[B A]` (`[(u0) (n 1)]`), so `B = cc-2`; the lift numbers and the","-  receiver recomputes the same root (`9600fe01…`, the review's digest).","-  The earlier text's \"at any fixpoint of any run\" was wrong and is","-  replaced in D5, lemma D and A1.","-  The predicate, in one sentence: *a state is refused under D5 iff the","-  root run, or a component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint at which","-  two elements of one guest set or map have equal keys and are connected","-  by a chain of shared unnumbered cursor cells through tied elements.*","+**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's","+slots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered","+repeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows","+with structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's","+`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the","+key; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;","+an install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is","+entered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an","+entry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the","+walk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups","+within one container in key order)."," ","-  **Exactly which structures that is.** A D5 state needs two or more","-  cursor cells with *equal bodies* (same stream identity and descriptor,","-  same position) that are held *only* through guest sets or maps, in","-  elements that each mention more than one of them, with equal keys. The","-  smallest are the directed 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` and the directed","-  3-cycle `#{[A B] [B C] [C A]}` (both interchangeable: the swap and the","-  rotation are automorphisms, so any entering order gives the same rows,","-  and 6a numbers them); the path `#{[A B] [B C]}` and the seven-cell","-  triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-404`) are refused and","-  genuinely need a tie-break (§6: two and four distinct roots under","-  host-order entry); the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`","-  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`) numbers `L = cc-1` by its key and conflicts","-  on `{A, B}`. One register, environment binding, pending slot or vector","-  position holding any one of the cells breaks the tie for the whole","-  component (`[#{X Y} X]`, the witness above). **Are they common in","-  realistic suspended tasks?** Unmeasured: the repository has no","-  version-3 task corpus (the Stage E scenarios and the v2 handoff census","-  tests hold cursor cells but no v3 lift exists before 6b). The structural","-  argument is that a cursor cell is created into a register","-  (`:stream/cursor` delivers to `rd`, engine `:2595-2602`) and reaches a","-  guest set only by guest code that builds a set of tuples of cursors on","-  one stream at one position and then drops every other reference; of","-  the repository's own fixtures only the two built for the purpose hit","-  D5, and under a tie-biased generator the rate is 35 of 2300 (§6).","-  Because the rate is unmeasured and 6a numbers the 2-cycle and 3-cycle,","-  this note does not leave the regression as a documented limitation:","-  **D6** (§2) is the minimum extra rule that repairs it, a search over","-  the conflicting component only (two trials for a 2-cycle, three for a","-  3-cycle, eleven for the seven-cell state), prototyped and measured in","-  §6: with D6 every one of the 2300 generated states numbers, the 2-cycle","-  and 3-cycle reach 6a's exact roots, and no D1–D4 numbering changes.","-  The owner's choice between D5 (refuse, no search anywhere) and D6","-  (bounded residue search) is §10 question 1; this note recommends D6.","+**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a","+`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell","+receives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell","+is passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the","+census row stays the contents table."," ","-**F5. The scheme is canonical.** Proved in §3: the output (rows or","-refusal) is a function of the abstract task up to isomorphism (renaming","-of cursor cells, permutation of every unordered container and of the","-census) and terminating; the work is accounted by the budget of §2","-\"Work\".","+**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in","+order: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and","+treated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties","+re-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same","+queue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups","+remain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie","+test."," ","-**F6. Prototype evidence (§6).** 2000 random value states (one to six","-cursor cells over two bodies, so ties are common; nested vectors, sets,","-maps with cursor-cell keys, sets of cursor-cell pairs including aliases","-such as `[X X]`, sets of sets, cells aliased across containers), 200","-frame-bearing states (act window, K-frame window, environments, a parked","-state) and 100 states with install children, each compared with itself","-renamed and shuffled and decoded through 6a's decoder: 0 invariance","-failures and 0 decode failures in either mode; D5 refuses 35 of 2300, D6","-refuses none; the largest `:work` charged is 1470 of 6a's default","-200000 and the largest trial count 13 of 10000. Every pin of rounds 1","-and 2 is unchanged (including under the rank spelling and with D6 on);","-the second review's digest for the witness is reproduced; the key bytes","-of one state under a 65536-character descriptor fell from 920118 (label","-spelling) to 1396 (rank spelling), the same as under a 128-character","-descriptor.","+### 1.3 The tie test"," ","-**F7. Portable ids minted in the VM are the wrong fix.** §5.","+A surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete","+(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members","+are pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any","+other surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered","+cell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells","+of each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group","+passes, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the","+host lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk","+returns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a","+**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`","+residue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only","+the member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state."," ","-## 2. The rule (replaces carrier §6 \"The procedure\")","+This is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the","+index-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;","+whatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but","+nothing it accepts can be host-order dependent."," ","-Input: the abstract task of carrier §6 (the §5 structure with cursor","-cells as identities, before numbering). Output: a bijection from the","-task's reached cursor cells to `:yin.k/cc-1 … :yin.k/cc-n`, or a","-refusal. Install children are numbered as their own tasks by the same","-rule (task boundary, unchanged), in any order, since scopes are disjoint.","+### 1.4 Refusal, and decode"," ","-**Spelling (the content encoding `CE`).** A run spells the item it has","-traversed under its final numbering. The root run spells a cursor cell","-numbered `k` as `[:cursor-cell [:n k]]`, `k` an exact integer; a local","-run spells a cursor cell it numbered itself (index `j`, counted from the","-enclosing run's local counter) as `[:cursor-cell [:u j r]]`, `r` the","-cell's body rank (an exact integer; rank order equals body-label order,","-so no comparison and no numbering changes against spelling the label,","-and the key's size no longer depends on the descriptor), and a cursor","-cell numbered by an enclosing run as that run spelled it. Nothing else","-changes from carrier §6 step 2: `[:lit d]` as itself, `[:row B]`","-expanded in place, structural data as itself, the census as","-`[:cursor-cells]`, an install child as `[:child]` (replacing step 2's","-`[:child <child root id>]`: a child sits only under a name-keyed install","-entry, never inside an unordered container, so its content never decides","-an order, and the encoding stays hash-free). Keyed structural rows are","-spelled in their canonical key order, `:window` in numeric register","-order; a `:set` or `:map` is spelled as the bytewise sorted vector of its","-elements' spellings (an entry as `[:entry K V]`), duplicates retained,","-where a tie group the run could not resolve is spelled in place as","-`[:tie K … K]` (its common key, once per element). All comparisons are","-bytewise on canonical CBOR of these synthetic vectors. `CE` is hash-free.","+The root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind","+`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container","+holding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under","+a member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by","+`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for","+an install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and","+`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing","+groups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up","+to renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.","+This is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`."," ","-**D1. Traversal.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the run's root item. A","-row's slots are visited in table order. Ordered repeated slots","-(`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order. Keyed rows with structural","-keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's `installs` and","-`module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the key, the order","-§5 emits; `:window` in ascending numeric register order as the carrier","-specifies. The census is not entered; an install child's `:task` row is","-not entered. A `:set`'s elements and a `:map`'s entries are each given a","-**key**: the spelling of a key run over the element under the numbering","-at the moment the container is entered (every element keyed from that","-one numbering). Elements are then taken in bytewise key order: a run of","-equal keys is a **tie group**, appended to the run's worklist (deferral","-order is canonical: containers in traversal order, groups within one","-container in key order); every other element is entered in key order,","-an entry's key before its value.","+The receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every","+install child, re-emits and compares root ids","+(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's","+`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);","+the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side","+`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes","+`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed","+by the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a","+resource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,","+since step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:","+numbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`","+(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`)."," ","-**D2. Meeting.** When the traversal reaches an identity position (a","-`[:cursor-cell C]` value or a `C` slot of a `:pending` row) whose cursor","-cell has no number, the cursor cell receives `|N| + 1` (the root run","-counts from 1; a local run counts its own cells from the enclosing run's","-local counter, so a nested local run never reuses an index of its","-enclosing run). An already numbered cursor cell is passed over. There is","-no first-occurrence spelling: every occurrence emits","-`[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]` and the census row (carrier §3) stays the","-contents table.","+### 1.5 Budgets and gates"," ","-**D3. Fixpoint.** After the traversal, the worklist's groups are","-re-examined in deferral order: each group's elements are re-keyed under","-the current numbering and taken as in D1 (singletons entered, equal runs","-re-deferred in order). Repeat while a round numbers at least one cursor","-cell and groups remain. Entering an element inside a group runs D1 on","-it, so a group discovered *inside* an entered element (`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`)","-joins the same worklist and is re-examined in the next round; there is","-one worklist per run, and the root run's worklist collects deferred","-groups from every depth.","+- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,","+  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).","+- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's","+  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).","+- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,","+  the count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key","+  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a","+  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation","+  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's bound of","+  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`: 128 per scalar and per","+  container, 4 per character, 16 plus length per byte payload), which","+  bounds the encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by","+  reference, so a spelling's own size is its spelled positions. Hence","+  every byte the procedure allocates for a comparison is charged before","+  the allocation, and the bytes alive at any time are bounded by the","+  budget plus the input. No polynomial bound is claimed; the budget is","+  the bound. Default `4194304` (it takes over the scale of the deleted","+  `:label-bytes`; appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300 generated states,","+  544554 for a 65536-character stream descriptor, 37210 for the pinned","+  fixture). A literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest","+  set or map exceeds it (6a's `:label-bytes` allowed about 8000); in an","+  ordered position a literal is never spelled and costs one unit.","+- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted."," ","-**D4. Components.** When a fixpoint is reached with groups remaining,","-link two tied elements (of any groups) that share an unnumbered cursor","-cell; a connected set of tied elements is a **component**. (At a","-fixpoint every unnumbered cursor cell occurs only inside tied elements,","-so all of its occurrences lie in one component.) Encode each component","-by a component run over the synthetic ordered row","-`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` in group order, `gᵢ` the group's deferral","-sequence number (a datum) and `iᵢ` the component's one element of that","-group, or, when the component holds several elements of group `gᵢ`, a","-synthetic `:set` of them (the D5/D6 case). A component run must be","-complete (no `[:tie]` in its spelling); it is incomplete only when it","-reaches a conflict of its own, which D5 or D6 decides. Sort the","-components by their encodings and **enter the run of least encoding**:","-each of its components has its component run's cursor cells numbered,","-in that run's local-index order, continuing the enclosing run's","-numbering; components of equal encoding are entered in host order (§3,","-lemma C: equal encodings are isomorphic and the result is the same).","-Remove the entered elements from their groups and return to D3. A group","-whose elements mention no unnumbered cursor cell holds equal values (the","-carrier refuses the duplicate at §8 step 5); its elements are entered in","-host order, which numbers nothing and emits identical rows.","+Exceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10","+(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality","+verdict, at lift and at decode alike."," ","-**D5. Conflict, and refusal without D6.** A component **conflicts** when","-it holds two elements of one group. Without D6, a conflict reached by the","-root run, or by a component run (directly, or through a nested component","-run of its own), refuses the lift: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind","-`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container","-holding the least conflicting group (for an install child, prefixed","-`[12 <install name> …]`), and `:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered","-cursor cells of every conflicting component at that fixpoint (so the","-diagnostic, like the verdict, does not depend on host order). A key run","-never refuses (§0; F4). A receiver that reaches D5 while recomputing","-refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. Neither is","-a resource refusal and neither is `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.","+### 1.6 Totality"," ","-**D6. Residue search (recommended; the repair of F4's regression).** A","-conflict is resolved instead of refused: let `g` be the least conflicting","-group of the component and `E` the component's elements of `g`. For each","-`e ∈ E` run a **trial**: a component run over the same `[:component …]`","-row in which `e` is entered first when its container is keyed, before","-its siblings are keyed (so `e`'s cursor cells take the first local","-indices and its siblings are keyed against them), and which runs to","-completion by D1–D6. Each trial charges `:search` one unit. The","-component's encoding is the least trial spelling, and entering the","-component adopts that trial's numbering (D4). Equal least spellings are","-isomorphic and either may be adopted (§3, lemma E). With D6 no","-structural refusal remains: the only refusals of the procedure are the","-resource refusals of \"Work\" below, and `:unresolved-cursor-cells` is not","-defined. With D6 in force, 6a's `:search` budget key stays and counts","-trials; without it, the key is deleted.","+The root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the","+implementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the","+census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk","+reaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the","+missing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered","+carrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`","+(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and","+expects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it."," ","-**Totality.** The root run ends with an empty worklist or a refusal.","-Before emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a","-bijection from the census's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation is a","-defect (an exception naming the missing or extra cursor cells), never a","-refusal, and never a partially numbered carrier.","+## 2. Proof of canonicality"," ","-**Memoization (implementation note, not part of the definition).** A","-local run's result is a function of its item, of the enclosing numbering","-restricted to the item's cursor cells, of the enclosing local counter and","-(D6) of the forced element; an implementation may memoize on that key and","-must return the numbering restricted to the item's own cursor cells (the","-prototype's one bug during round 2 was returning the whole numbering of","-another element's context, which the decode round trip caught). The memo","-holds only spellings that were paid for under `:work`, so it adds no","-unaccounted bytes. No bound on the number of distinct memo contexts is","-claimed; the memo is an optimization and the budget is the bound.","+Call two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**","+when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the","+host order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and","+queues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic","+configurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits","+byte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure","+`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`."," ","-**Validation and decode.** Unchanged in form: the receiver decodes, runs","-the root run, re-emits and compares root ids (`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`","-on mismatch). In 6a's `decode*` the recomputation is `emit` itself","-(`rows.cljc:570-575`), which numbers the root *and every install child*","-(`emit-node`, `:185-189`); the **whole** of that call is wrapped so that","-only a lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` of kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`","-(D5, from the root or from a child) is translated to `:yin.k/undecodable`","-kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed by the","-carried root id (a child's path then reads `[<root id> 12 <name> …]`);","-a resource refusal (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit`) propagates as","-itself; no other non-portable kind can arise there, because step 5 has","-already checked literals and markers. The second review's escape (an","-install child's D5 leaving `decode*` as `:yin.k/non-portable` with path","-`[:child 15]`) came from translating only the root's numbering; the","-prototype now wraps the whole recomputation and the case is in §6. The","-validator order stays: step 6 (numbering) after step 5 and before","-`validate-frames` (`rows.cljc:576-578`; test","-`numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `rows_test.cljc:206`). With D6 the","-translation has nothing to translate and is omitted.","+*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the","+numbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,","+ranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts","+(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of","+their members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,","+so the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the","+order in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie","+step below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a","+function of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean","+isomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is","+faithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and","+count, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under","+which the members agree)."," ","-**Work.** No polynomial bound is claimed. The second review showed the","-round-2 bound counted positions but not bytes (a 65536-character stream","-descriptor multiplied key bytes by 170 at fixed `P`, `d`, `n`, `m`) and","-that memoization's benefit across differing local contexts is unproved.","-The procedure is instead *accounted*, as 6a already accounts refinement:","-the `:work` budget is charged one unit per position visited (D1, D3,","-every local run) and one unit per position spelled plus one unit per","-byte of literal or structural datum copied into a spelling, before the","-copy; `:search` (D6) is charged one unit per trial; `:depth` bounds the","-structural depth as today, `:cursor-cells` bounds `n`, `:rows` the","-carried rows and `:bytes` the carried bytes. Exceeding any budget is the","-resource refusal of carrier §10.10 (`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit","-[<key>]`), never a canonicality verdict, exactly as 6a's `:label-bytes`","-was. The budget charges every allocated spelling byte before allocation,","-so the bytes materialized at any time are bounded by `:work` plus the","-carried bytes. What the rank spelling guarantees is narrower and exact:","-a key's bytes are a function of the element's expanded structure and of","-the number of distinct bodies, not of the bodies' sizes (§6: 1396 key","-bytes at both 128 and 65536 descriptor characters). The crude bound","-without memoization is `O(P · (n + 1)^(d + 1) · log m)` positions and","-`O(B)` bytes per spelling (`P` expanded positions counted per reference,","-`B` expanded bytes, `d` the unordered-container nesting depth, `n` cursor","-cells, `m` the largest container), which the budget enforces rather than","-the analysis. Measured: every fixture of §6 charges under 1000 `:work`","-units, the 2300 generated states at most 1470, against 6a's default","-200000; the seven-cell state charges 488 and 11 trials under D6. The","-`:label-bytes` budget disappears with the growing labels.","+*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the","+queue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the","+surviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to","+isomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key","+completeness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural."," ","-## 3. Proof of canonicality","+*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal","+and complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection","+`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of","+distinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state","+(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by","+the identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the","+members of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders","+`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers","+agree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the","+same block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of","+the procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are","+the same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering","+all of them before the next pass changes nothing."," ","-Call two configurations (abstract task, partial numbering, worklist)","-**isomorphic** when a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the","-other: the structures agree up to renaming by `π` and up to the host","-order of every unordered container and of the census, numbers agree","-(`N'(π(c)) = N(c)`), and worklists correspond group by group in order.","-The proof is a simultaneous induction over runs, ordered by (number of","-unnumbered cursor cells of the run's item, nesting depth of the item):","-a key run, a component run or a trial is over an item with no more","-unnumbered cells than its enclosing run and strictly fewer nested","-unordered containers, or (a trial) strictly fewer unnumbered cells; so","-the lemmas below may assume themselves for every local run a run spawns.","+*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is","+sorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so","+isomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎"," ","-**Lemma A (keys are canonical).** The key of an element is the spelling","-of a key run over it, and a key run reads only structure, body ranks","-(the rank of a body is a function of the census up to isomorphism) and","-numbers; its own listed-order inputs are (i) element order inside nested","-containers, which it never uses except through keys, by the induction,","-(ii) the host order of components of equal encoding in its D4 step,","-covered by lemma C, and (iii) nothing else: a key run that reaches a","-conflict does not choose, it spells the residue `[:tie K … K]`, which is","-a function of the group's common key and size. So isomorphic items under","-corresponding numberings have byte-identical keys, and the sorted order","-of a container's elements is a function of the configuration up to","-isomorphism.","+*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is","+the key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks","+nest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every","+W3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers","+a cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves","+(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier","+refuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step","+and the budget bounds the count."," ","-**Lemma B (ties are exactly what keys cannot separate).** By","-construction: elements are deferred iff their keys are equal bytes.","+Consequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host","+enumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of","+containers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are","+byte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing","+survives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the","+cross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before."," ","-**Lemma C (components).** At a fixpoint let `C₁ … Cₘ` be the components","-of one run of equal encodings, each encoding complete, and let","-`U₁ … Uₘ` be their unnumbered cursor cells. The `Uᵢ` are pairwise","-disjoint and each `Uᵢ` is confined to `Cᵢ` (every occurrence of an","-unnumbered cell lies in a tied element, hence in its own component).","-Equal, complete encodings of `[:component g₁ i₁ …]` rows are a faithful","-spelling (literals as themselves, rows expanded, every cursor cell by","-index and body rank, groups by sequence number, a group's several","-elements as a sorted set), so they exhibit, for each pair `Cᵢ, Cⱼ`, a","-bijection `Uᵢ → Uⱼ` under which the components are the same structure","-group by group. Entering the run in host order `σ` gives the cells of","-`C_{σ(t)}` the numbers `b + (t−1)·|U| + 1 …` by local index; in order","-`σ'` the cells of `C_{σ'(t)}` receive the same numbers. The block-swap","-bijection composed from the pairwise bijections is an isomorphism of the","-two resulting configurations: numbers agree by construction, structures","-agree because the swapped components are isomorphic and nothing outside","-them mentions their cells, and the worklists agree because the same","-groups remain, with the same elements removed. ∎","+## 3. What is refused"," ","-**Lemma D (the D5 verdict is invariant).** Whether the root run, or a","-component run it requires, reaches a fixpoint with a conflicting","-component depends only on which tied elements share unnumbered cursor","-cells at that fixpoint, which is a function of the configuration up to","-isomorphism (lemma A gives the ties, the sharing relation is structural);","-and whether a component run is complete is the same question one level","-down, by the induction. Key runs do not enter the verdict: their","-conflicts are spelled, not refused, and the spelling is canonical (lemma","-A iii).","+Refused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some","+surviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:"," ","-**Lemma E (D6 trials).** At a conflicting component the set `E` of","-elements of its least conflicting group is a function of the","-configuration up to isomorphism (lemma D names the component and its","-groups canonically). Each trial is a deterministic function of (the","-configuration up to isomorphism, the chosen element), by the induction","-on the trial run, which has strictly fewer unnumbered cells than the","-enclosing run (its forced element holds at least one, being linked into","-the component by a shared unnumbered cell). So the multiset of trial","-spellings is canonical and its least member is canonical. Two trials","-with equal least spellings have produced isomorphic numberings of the","-component (faithful spelling, as in lemma C), so adopting either yields","-isomorphic configurations. ∎","+- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose","+  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]","+  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not","+  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key","+  alike).","+- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,","+  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`","+  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`","+  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream","+  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the","+  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is","+  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square","+  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it","+  without a search.","+- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another","+  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the","+  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`","+  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie","+  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused)."," ","-**Theorem.** The procedure's output on isomorphic configurations is the","-same: both refuse at corresponding points, or both emit byte-identical","-rows. *Proof.* By the induction above. D1 and D2 decisions are functions","-of the configuration up to isomorphism (lemma A); D3 only repeats D1 on","-deferred groups in a canonical order; D4's choice of the least run is","-canonical (lemma A on component encodings, which are complete by D5/D6)","-and its only listed-order input is harmless (lemma C); D5 is invariant","-(lemma D); D6 is invariant (lemma E). Emission spells cursor cells by","-number only and sorts every emitted unordered slot by canonical bytes","-under the final numbering (carrier §5), so isomorphic final","-configurations emit the same rows. ∎","+Equal bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor","+does not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the","+2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the","+mixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the","+two-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a","+refuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and","+numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers."," ","-Consequences:","+Measured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every","+listed order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,","+else 48 sampled orders):"," ","-1. *Function of structure, renaming-invariant, enumeration-invariant*:","-   the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of the containers, or","-   both. No decision reads a source id or a host order except at the","-   point lemma C covers.","-2. *Terminating.* The structural edge family is acyclic (carrier §8 step","-   3), so every traversal is finite. In any run each D3 round that is not","-   the last numbers a cursor cell; each D4 step numbers at least one","-   cursor cell or removes a duplicate group; each local run is over an","-   item with fewer unordered containers nested below it than its","-   spawning run's item, or (a trial) with fewer unnumbered cells, so the","-   induction measure of §3 decreases and there are finitely many local","-   runs. The budget of §2 \"Work\" is what bounds the count.","-3. *Deterministic*, hence two lifts of one state are byte-identical and","-   the receiver's recomputation is well defined.","-4. *Sharing survives*: the numbering is a bijection on reached cursor","-   cells (D2 never renumbers; totality is asserted), every occurrence of","-   one cursor cell spells one number, and two cursor cells at one","-   position get two numbers.","-5. *Cross-engine law*: two engines lifting one canonical frame produce","-   abstract tasks equal up to renaming (the frame is engine-independent,","-   continuation §2.1) and therefore, by the theorem, equal rows.","-6. *D6 moves no D1–D4 id.* Trials run only where D4 found a conflict,","-   which without D6 is a refusal; every numbering D1–D4 assign is","-   identical with and without D6 (§6 checks the pins both ways).","+| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |","+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|","+| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |","+| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |","+| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |","+| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |"," ","-## 4. Amendments","+Every refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and","+decode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no","+version-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies."," ","+**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a","+fixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.","+","+## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept","+","+Against `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and","+`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).","+","+**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on","+labels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation","+bound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label","+seeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`","+(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`","+(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers","+(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`","+and `:search`.","+","+**rows.cljc, rewritten.**","+- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract","+  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census","+  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence","+  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the","+  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections","+  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"","+  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as","+  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;","+  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32","+  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);","+  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three","+  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's","+  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.","+- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row","+  added (`[:V :V]`).","+- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.","+- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal","+  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.","+- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the","+  whole `emit` call.","+- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →","+  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".","+","+**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,","+`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/","+`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),","+`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),","+`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,","+`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after","+`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,","+`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`","+keys with their defaults.","+","+**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`","+(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,","+`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,","+`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),","+`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,","+`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),","+`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`","+(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,","+`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and","+`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),","+`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests","+(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`","+(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`","+(`:392-397`).","+","+**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**","+- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)","+  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on","+  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become","+  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.","+- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`","+  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the","+  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`","+  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the","+  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.","+- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →","+  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable","+  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the","+  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.","+- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →","+  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census","+  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an","+  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no","+  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`","+  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the","+  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal","+  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit","+  [:work]` under the default budget.","+","+## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering","+","+12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns","+    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review","+    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness","+    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two","+    frame fixtures.","+13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review","+    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's","+    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned","+    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.","+14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,","+    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and","+    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,","+    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.","+15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and","+    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,","+    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers","+    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.","+16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the","+    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,","+    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle","+    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`","+    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the","+    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of","+    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable","+    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never","+    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the","+    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its","+    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with","+    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.","+17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`","+    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,","+    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;","+    `55ff168e…` removed.","+18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix","+    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with","+    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and","+    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every","+    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict","+    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is","+    reported.","+19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach","+    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.","+20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,","+    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,","+    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,","+    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and","+    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the","+    rewritten tests of §4 pass.","+21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit","+    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under","+    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses","+    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor","+    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at","+    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.","+22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default","+    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`","+    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.","+","+## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents","+"," **A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace","-with §2 above (vocabulary, the spelling, D1–D6, totality, work). Keep the","-section's first paragraph (the counterexample) and amend \"What is","-defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical numbering is defined","-as the output of the discovery-order procedure below, which is a function","-of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host enumeration of guest","-sets, maps and the census (proof in","-`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §3). It is not the","-minimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the","-traversal cannot make by itself, between two equal-keyed elements of one","-guest set or map that are linked by shared unnumbered cursor cells at a","-fixpoint of the root run or of a component run, is resolved by a search","-confined to that component (D6) [or, in the D5 variant: is refused (D5),","-not searched; the refusal certifies nothing about interchangeability and","-can be narrowed later without changing any numbering assigned today].\"","-State in step 2's replacement that `[:n k]` and `[:u j r]` carry exact","-CBOR integers, that `r` is the body rank, and that `[:child]` replaces","-`[:child <child root id>]` (reason in §2 \"Spelling\").","+with §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)","+and amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical","+numbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure","+below, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host","+enumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in","+`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the","+minimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk","+cannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that","+the tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused","+(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about","+interchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any","+numbering assigned today.\""," "," **A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two"," sentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is"]},{"oldStart":532,"oldLines":449,"newStart":485,"newLines":264,"lines":[" to the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is"," thereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are"," invariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and","-census (proof in the numbering note, §3); the procedure's one use of host","-order, entering components of equal encoding in the order listed, cannot","-change the rows (lemma C), and a receiver never decides validity by its","-own enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a","-lift kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the","+census (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,","+entering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot","+change the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own","+enumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift","+kind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the"," procedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before"," any attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and","-never accepts an unfinished check [D5 variant: ; a receiver that reaches","-D5 refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`].\"","+never accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie","+refusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\""," ","-**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** Replace \"The class-erased skeletons of §6","-are used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order","-of an emitted row\" with \"The spellings (content encodings) of §6 are","-used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of","-an emitted row\".","+**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used","+only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an","+emitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the","+numbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\"."," "," **A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the"," root and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind","-`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the","-bound is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" D5 variant: add","-\"`:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if D5 is reached","-in the root or in a child, translated from the recomputation's lift-side","-refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed by the carried root id\";","-and in the diagnostics paragraph after step 12 add","-`:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds, change","-\"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and","-`:metadata`\" to \"the three kinds …, `:metadata` and","-`:unresolved-cursor-cells`\", and in §11 \"two lift kinds and two","-`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" to \"three … and three …\". With D6","-these counts are unchanged.","+`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie","+refusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the","+recomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed","+by the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound","+is exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics","+paragraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the","+`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF","+§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,","+`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and","+two `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\"."," "," **A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant"," refinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which"," is invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound"," on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work"," and search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,","-traversal depth, traversal-and-spelling work and (D6) residue-search","-trials for §6 (its `:work` unit counts positions and copied bytes, so","-spellings are paid for before they are allocated)\"; and \"never falls","-back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes host enumeration order for","-the canonical traversal\". Proposals list: \"as defined by its procedure\"","-→ \"as defined by discovery order [D5 variant: , and the D5 refusal","-class]\".","+traversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit","+counts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling","+is encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes","+host enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as","+defined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the","+`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\"."," "," **A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's"," canonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical","-traversal)\".","+walk)\"."," ","-**A7 — continuation §2.2.** Replace \"numbered by the draft's §6","-procedure\" with \"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by","+**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →","+\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by"," `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\"."," ","-**A8 — continuation §5.5.** Three replacements. (i) \"Draft §6 is","-invariant under renaming of the **cursor cells** (its labels never encode","-an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only other identity it consumes is","-the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label","+**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it","+consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label"," (`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and","-in cursor-cell bodies\" → \"Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the","-**cursor cells** (it reads no incoming cursor-cell id), and the only","-other identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's","-body label (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6","-as amended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\".","-(ii) \"Unordered containers (`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the","-parked table) are encoded by sorting their elements' encodings bytewise","-(draft §6 step 2), never by host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps","-are entered in the bytewise order of their elements' spellings, and","-`:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in the byte order of their","-structural keys (draft §6 D1), never in host enumeration order\". (iii)","-Leave \"never derived from traversal order\" but qualify it: \"never","-derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical traversal of draft §6","-D1 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".","+in cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is","+the stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream","+identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank","+inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers","+(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by","+sorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by","+host traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise","+order of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked","+table in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in","+host enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →","+\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft","+§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\"."," ","-**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items 12–22","-of §9 below as the acceptance of the rewritten numbering.","+**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items","+12–22 of §5."," ","-**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change: \"minted by the emitter (stable within","-one lift; remapped on lowering) and not content-addressed\" remains true.","+**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change."," ","-## 5. The alternative: portable ids minted in the VM","+## 7. Owner questions"," ","-The owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from","-creation so the carrier sends it as is. To work across engines the id","-must be computable by every engine from shared inputs. The candidates","-(branch `srvm-phase6a`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) are: the task's id","-counter (`gen-id`, `:227-230`; `gensym`, `:2312-2318`, which lower adopts","-as `max(local, carried)`), the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`","-executions (`handle-cursor-unminted`'s `seq-n`, `:557-568`, minted at the","-`:stream/cursor` effect, `:2595-2602`, with the id from `gensym` and the","-sealed reference from `issue-ref`, `:311-323`), a hash of (occurrence,","-counter), or a random id. Every one of them names *history*, not","-structure:","+1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its","+   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from","+   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a","+   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the","+   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A","+   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random","+   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the","+   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states","+   numbered, different pins)?","+2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus","+   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?"," ","-- *Same structure, same hash fails.* Hold every carried field equal:","-  task A executes `:stream/cursor` three times (ids `cursor-0`,","-  `cursor-1`, `cursor-2`; counter 3) and drops the second; task B executes","-  `:stream/cursor` twice and then consumes one unrelated fresh name","-  (`gensym` for a non-cursor id; counter 3) and keeps both. The two","-  snapshots are structurally equal (two cursor cells with equal bodies at","-  equal positions, the same id counter, the same everything else), yet A","-  carries `{cursor-0, cursor-2}` and B `{cursor-0, cursor-1}`, so the root","-  ids differ. Any id recomputed from the snapshot's *structure* instead","-  is the canonicalization problem of §2 again, not an alternative to it.","-- *The cross-engine harness law* would hold only if both engines agree","-  on the minting history, which makes an execution trace part of the","-  frame's identity; a random or occurrence-bound id breaks it outright","-  (a fork halt has no occurrence; a child has none of its own).","-- *The VM grows a second name.* Lower mints a fresh private resource","-  entry per cursor cell (carrier §8 step 12) and must keep the carried","-  id beside it; a relift after further execution must mint ids that","-  cannot collide with carried ones, which needs an (origin, counter)","-  pair: a global naming scheme for a thing that only needs a name","-  relative to its state.","+---"," ","-Discovery order answers the owner's observation differently: the","-identity stays local and the carrier gives it a *structural* name, the","-n-th cursor cell met, exactly as a de Bruijn index names a binder by","-position rather than by a global symbol. Recommend against portable","-ids for immutable creation-history ids carried unchanged; that is the","-only kind proposed.","+# Appendices (non-normative)"," ","-## 6. Prototype","+## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)"," ","-`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (not repo code; a","-scratch project under `collab/`, never committed) implements D1–D6 as","-one schema-driven run procedure (root run, key runs, component runs and","-trials share the code; a local run differs only in its number tag, in","-spelling residues as `[:tie]` and, for a trial, in its forced element),","-memoized as §2 describes, charging `:work` and `:search` as §2 \"Work\"","-describes, with the totality assertion, an emitter that numbers install","-children by the same procedure, a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`","-with its numbering check off and then wraps the whole discovery","-recomputation (root and children) in the narrow translation, and two","-switches: `:residue-search` (D6; off is the D5 variant) and","-`:permissive` (diagnostic: a conflict is resolved by one host-first","-trial, to test whether a refused state is in fact interchangeable). Run","-from the probe directory (JVM only; `@#'rows/…` reaches 6a's private","-helpers); the `clojure` launcher was not available to this session, so","-the run below used the launcher's cached classpath directly:","+`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under","+`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure","+used for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering","+restricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the","+charging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install","+children by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`","+with its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation","+in §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing","+group is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of","+refusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact","+interchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when","+there are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling","+false` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype","+is kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from","+the probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match","+`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):"," "," ```","-java @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"","+mise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\""," ```"," ","-Results of 2026-10-12, round 3 (full log `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`;","-6a's own tests pass unchanged in the same project):","+Results of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's","+own tests pass unchanged in the same pro